<h1 class="article-title">Developers Sought for Two Historically Significant Properties in Karosta Industrial Park</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The historic buildings on Zemgales Street have been designated as architecturally and culturally significant urban heritage. Reconstruction must preserve structural and finishing solutions, existing building volumes, original window divisions and smooth sheet-metal roofing. The maximum building height is three storeys. At the same time, the properties lie within Karosta Industrial Park and are zoned for mixed development with an industrial function.</p><p> </p><p>“This is a logical step in continuing the development of Karosta Industrial Park. Having seen and assessed the office and production building of EB Liepāja, commissioned at the end of 2022, where Karosta’s historic architecture was successfully restored and integrated, other businesses have also shown interest,” says Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis. He stresses that development is easier when there are tangible examples to see. “I am very pleased that several other companies are interested in incorporating Karosta’s historic buildings into their development projects, including production facilities and logistics centres. An auction is a good way to identify an investor so that two more historic buildings on Zemgales Street can be restored in the coming years.”</p><p> </p><p>The principal permitted use of the land and buildings is multifunctional: low-impact manufacturing, commercial premises, retail outlets and service facilities. Public buildings are also permitted, but residential development is not allowed.</p><p>To encourage the restoration of historic buildings or their integration into new developments, the auction rules require the successful bidder to obtain confirmation that design conditions have been met within 24 months of the purchase agreement taking effect. Construction specified in the auction rules and agreement must begin within 25 months and be completed within 48 months of that date.</p><p> </p><p>EB Liepāja recognised Liepāja as an industrial city that has always respected cultural and historical heritage, integrating this heritage into its production building by combining Karosta’s historic architecture with modern industry and creating higher-value manufacturing space. EB Liepāja board member Māris Pētersons previously stated that the modern factory would not have been built without the supportive attitude and involvement of the municipality and the Liepāja SEZ Authority. Construction was carried out by UPTK.</p><p> </p><p>Background:</p><p>The idea for Karosta Industrial Park emerged in 1999, aiming to give a former military base a new purpose, integrate it into the city, create jobs, increase tax revenue for city and state budgets, and make more funding available for education, culture, sport and other projects. Building new factories in Karosta remained impractical or unattractive to investors until 2015, when the municipality and the Liepāja SEZ Authority developed almost 20 hectares of the area for business use.</p></div>
</div>
